{"id": "f9f4e4ac-8869-4d05-97cd-9ebe89864386", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll scan IAM across your agent space's accounts for long-lived credentials. That covers access keys, Bedrock API keys and other service-specific credentials. I'll check which ones can reach Bedrock, when each was last used, and whether anything depends on them. I'll report back with a removal recommendation for each.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_sLZjsjUWElbsj1wT00YDOp", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-stale-credentials-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Audit long-lived credentials that grant Amazon Bedrock access and identify those NOT used in the last 90 days. Then classify each as safe-to-remove or not, with reasoning. READ-ONLY: do not modify anything.",
  "context": "User wants to clean up stale long-lived credentials with Bedrock access. Current time is 2026-11-24T19:08:00Z, so the 90-day cutoff is 2026-08-26T19:08:00Z. Long-lived credentials include: (1) IAM user access keys (AKIA...), (2) Bedrock API keys, which are IAM service-specific credentials with ServiceName bedrock.amazonaws.com (list via iam list_service_specific_credentials per user; also check the ServiceCredentialAlias/ExpirationDate and status), and (3) any other IAM user login/service-specific credentials tied to Bedrock access. Short-lived/role-based credentials are out of scope. Use list_associations to discover all AWS accounts in this agent space and scan each (home account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1; IAM is global but note Bedrock usage can occur in any region).",
  "resources": ["All IAM users in every associated account", "Access keys", "Service-specific credentials for bedrock.amazonaws.com"],
  "timeframe": "Last 90 days: 2026-08-26T19:08:00Z to 2026-11-24T19:08:00Z",
  "criteria": "For each IAM user: determine whether it has Bedrock access via attached/inline policies, group policies, or permissions boundaries (actions like bedrock:*, bedrock:InvokeModel*, bedrock:Converse*, AmazonBedrockFullAccess, AmazonBedrockLimitedAccess/API-key managed policies, or wildcard * admin access that includes Bedrock). Then for each credential report: user, account, credential type and id (access key id or service-specific credential id, never secrets), status (Active/Inactive), creation date, last-used date, last-used service/region (iam get_access_key_last_used; for service-specific credentials use ServiceSpecificCredential metadata / credential report / user last-activity and, if needed, CloudTrail via lookup_cloudtrail_events for bedrock events by that principal or key id), and how Bedrock access is granted. Flag a credential as stale if never used or last used before 2026-08-26. Safe-to-remove assessment: mark SAFE when it is stale (or never used and older than 90 days) and nothing suggests dependency; mark CAUTION when ambiguous, e.g. it is the user's only credential, user is a service/CI account, tags/description suggest a seasonal or DR/break-glass purpose, last-used data is unavailable or tracking began recently, key created less than 90 days ago, or it is shared; mark NOT SAFE if used within 90 days. Note caveats: IAM last-used info is only tracked per service and may reflect non-Bedrock use, so a key used recently for a different service but with Bedrock access is still 'in use'. Also note the likely least-risk approach (deactivate first, observe, then delete). Return a concise table plus a short summary of counts, and list any accounts/users you could not access."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit IAM credentials with Bedrock access unused for 90 days"}], "created_at": null}

